
  [1mThe fork:[0m what [3mkinds[0m of checks the shared layer has to support decides the shape of a validator, and two of the four
  options are one-way doors.

  - [1mPresence / required[0m — a rule sees one field's value, returns ok or a message. Simplest possible signature. Cheap to
    add to later.
  - [1mFormat & length[0m (email, min length, numeric range) — still one value in, one result out. Same signature as presence,
    just more rule types. Also cheap to add later.
  - [1mCross-field[0m (confirm-password matches password, end-date after start-date) — a rule can no longer see just its own
    value; it needs the whole form. This changes the validator signature for [3mevery[0m rule, so retrofitting it means
    touching all of them. Not cheap to add later.
  - [1mAsync / server-checked[0m (is this username taken?) — the result becomes a promise, so submit handling becomes async
    and the form needs a pending state. This is the most expensive one to retrofit, because it changes the calling code
    in every form, not just the rules.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m tell me which of these you actually need. If cross-field or async are in the plan at all, I'd
  design the signature for them now even if the first forms don't use them — those two are the retrofits that hurt. If
  they're genuinely not coming, leaving them out keeps this to a very small module, and I'd rather not build a rule
  engine for forms that may never exist.
